      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of one foot, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches.\n2. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot.\n3. Find the ratio of these volumes.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches. The formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a cube is given by:\n\n\\[\nV = s^3\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( s \\) is the edge length of the cube. For a cube with an edge length of six inches:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{6 inches}} = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot. The formula for the volume of a cube remains the same, but we need to ensure that the units are consistent. Since one foot is equal to 12 inches, the edge length in inches is:\n\n\\[\n1 \\text{ foot} = 12 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nSo, the volume \\( V \\) of the cube with an edge length of 12 inches is:\n\n\\[\nV_{\\text{1 foot}} = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot. This ratio is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_{\\text{6 inches}}}{V_{\\text{1 foot}}} = \\frac{216}{1728}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. The GCD of 216 and 1728 is 216. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 216:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{216 \\div 216}{1728 \\div 216} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\n\\]",
