      "text": "To solve this problem, we first need to calculate the volume of each cube. The volume \\( V \\) of a cube with edge length \\( s \\) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\nV = s^3\n\\]\n\nGiven that the edge length of the first cube is 6 inches, its volume is:\n\n\\[\nV_1 = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the volume of the second cube with an edge length of 1 foot. Since 1 foot is equal to 12 inches, the edge length in inches is 12. Therefore, the volume of the second cube is:\n\n\\[\nV_2 = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the ratio of the volume of the first cube to the volume of the second cube. This ratio is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_1}{V_2} = \\frac{216}{1728}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. The GCD of 216 and 1728 is 216. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 216:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{216 \\div 216}{1728 \\div 216} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of 6 inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of 1 foot is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\n\\]",
